The goldens are the REFERENCE dazzle's output, and all five now agree with it
character for character — there is no deviation left to record.

History, because two of these lines were a recorded deviation for a while and
whoever re-reads them should know what closed it:

  useloss  the reference reported two extra ArcEngine diagnostics
           ("reference in architecture to non-existent ID").
  noparts  same cause: "element DSSSL-SPECIFICATION unfinished in meta-DTD".
  extspec  the missingPart anchor of a SPECID reference was 23 columns off.

All three came from the same place: the port kept the DSSSL architecture as a
GATE and walked the BASE events, where the reference's loadDoc is
ArcEngine::parseAll. Closed in three steps, 2026-08-09:

  1. the VALIDATING half of the architecture engine (the meta-DTD content check,
     the arch-space id table) — which is where both extra diagnostics come from;
  2. mungeMetaDtd plus the arch-space ENTITY/NOTATION attribute semantics,
     without which the generated stream carries no entity semantics and the
     `document`/`content` attributes below would resolve to nothing;
  3. the routing itself (DssslSpecEventHandler.route_through_arch).

★Step 3 also fixed extspec by making the anchor question moot in the right
direction: the GENERATED start-element event now carries the base event's MARKUP
location, which is what `event.location()` of a StartElementEvent is in the
reference. Reading the port's second location there put the diagnostic at the
attribute list instead of at the element.

Everything these fixtures are FOR — that a Location survives its parse and still
resolves to the right file, line and column — is therefore now proven against
the reference on both arena paths, and extok (a runtime error resolving through
an external specification's chunk table into an entity-expanded .scm) is
character-for-character equal.
